2. Why use a linked list?
A linked list is a linear data structure in which each node contains data and a pointer to the next node.

Unlike an array, a linked list does not require all its elements to occupy consecutive memory locations.

Structure of a call log node

Call Record

Phone number · Date/time · Duration · Call type

Next pointer

The next pointer stores the reference to the following node. The last node points to NULL.

Advantages
Dynamic memory allocation: nodes can be added as needed.

Easy insertion and deletion when the relevant node or position is known.

Merging can be performed by changing pointers rather than shifting array elements.

Suitable for practicing pointer manipulation and dynamic data structures.

Limitations
Accessing a particular record requires traversal from the head.

Each node requires extra memory for a pointer.

Searching for duplicates can be expensive without an additional index.

Incorrect pointer operations can cause lost nodes or memory errors.

3. Project objectives
The main objectives of the project are to:

Store call records in a linked list.

Insert new call records.

Display all call records.

Merge two call logs into a single list.

Search for a call by phone number.

Delete a call record.

Sort calls by date and time.

Optionally remove duplicate records.

Analyze the time and space complexity of each operation.

